// Title: a bar chart's #max of 0 (or a negative one, clamped to 0) draws
// every non-zero bar at full height, while a sparkline's non-positive #max
// scales to the data; the barchart pins use one bar and cannot tell.
//
// Command (from this directory):
//   timeout -s KILL 30 python3 -I tests-tui-pty-drive.py 4 4 tests-tui-barchart-max-zero.gx
// and, for the auto scale, the same program without `#max: &0,`.
//
// Expected (as sparkline's MaxV does: "positive and finite, else the data's
// maximum"): bar 5 at half the height of bar 10, as without #max:
//   ...\x1b[1;1H█\x1b[2;1H█\x1b[3;1H█\x1b[3;3H█\x1b[4;1H█\x1b[4;3H5...
// Observed: bar 5 full height, rows 1-3 of column 3 filled:
//   ...\x1b[1;1H█\x1b[1;3H█\x1b[2;1H█\x1b[2;3H█\x1b[3;1H█\x1b[3;3H█\x1b[4;1H█\x1b[4;3H5...
// barchart.rs passes `chart.max(0)`, which ratatui raises to 1.
use tui::barchart::{bar, bar_chart, bar_group};
bar_chart(#max: &0, &[bar_group([bar(&10), bar(&5)])])
